WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0043: horse races without a stopwatch,
a junior-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.100
Two parts: 36 horses and the top four,
then 30 horses and a plan for any case.

3
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:16.100
Part A: 36 horses, six in every race,
and we want the four fastest, in order.

4
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.700
The stable has 36 horses, each with a fixed speed.
Six run in every race.

5
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.700
There is no stopwatch: after a race
we see only the finishing order, no times.

6
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:30.100
The question: how many races at least
to know the top four for certain?

7
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.100
The idea: for every horse, count the horses
known for certain to be faster.

8
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:38.100
Indirectly too: if x beat y and y beat z,
x is faster than z, though they never met.

9
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:42.100
A horse with four or more known faster
cannot be in the top four. It is out.

10
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.800
Races 1 to 6: every horse must run,
so we race six groups of six.

11
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.600
Each row is a group, each column a place in it.
A1 won group A, A2 came second.

12
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.500
The same for the other five groups.

13
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:02.500
In every group, places 5 and 6 are out at once:
each has four known faster in its group.

14
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.700
Race 7: the six group winners.
Whoever wins here is the fastest horse.

15
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:13.933
C1 won, so it is place 1. Sort the rows
by the winners' race: C1's group on top.

16
00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:18.733
Now count each horse's known faster horses.
The number replaces the name.

17
00:01:18.733 --> 00:01:22.733
Place i in row j: i − 1 faster in its group,
plus j − 1 winners faster than its winner.

18
00:01:22.733 --> 00:01:27.733
Everyone at 4 or more is out:
14 more horses leave in this race.

19
00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:32.033
Exactly nine candidates are left, a staircase:
numbers 1, 2 and 3.

20
00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:37.433
Place 2 has only two candidates, C2 and F1:
each has one known faster horse, C1.

21
00:01:37.433 --> 00:01:41.600
For place 3, C3, F2 and B1 join,
with two known faster.

22
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.567
And for place 4, four more: C4, F3, B2 and E1,
with three known faster.

23
00:01:47.067 --> 00:01:52.300
Race 8: all five candidates for places 2 and 3,
plus E1, a candidate for place 4.

24
00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:56.700
F1 > F2 > B1 > E1 > C2 > C3.
They all ran together, so their order is known.

25
00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:01.700
So F1 is place 2 and F2 place 3:
no other horse can take those places.

26
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:06.700
Now C2, C3, C4, B2 and E1 have four
known faster horses, and they are out.

27
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:11.233
Two candidates are left for place 4:
B1, third in race 8, and F3.

28
00:02:11.233 --> 00:02:16.433
Race 9: B1 against F3. B1 wins, place 4.
Done after 9 races.

29
00:02:16.433 --> 00:02:20.333
The top four, in order:
C1, F1, F2 and B1.

30
00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:26.633
The page's box runs the same example,
hidden speeds, one race after another.

31
00:02:26.633 --> 00:02:31.433
After the six group races:
in every row, places 5 and 6 are already out.

32
00:02:31.433 --> 00:02:36.567
Race 7, the winners: the rows are sorted,
each cell shows its known faster count.

33
00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:40.967
Race 8: F1 is place 2 and F2 place 3,
and two candidates are left.

34
00:02:40.967 --> 00:02:46.100
And race 9 settles place 4, B1.
On the page you can draw other speeds too.

35
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:50.933
Why can't it be done in fewer than 9?
Let's go through it step by step.

36
00:02:50.933 --> 00:02:56.433
Every horse must run at least once,
or it might be the fastest. Six races.

37
00:02:56.433 --> 00:03:00.733
To know place 1, the winners must meet:
race 7.

38
00:03:00.733 --> 00:03:06.233
After seven races, in any plan, at least
nine candidates are left for places 2 to 4.

39
00:03:06.233 --> 00:03:10.267
One race runs only six of them,
and three stay outside.

40
00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:15.433
The adversary picks the result so that one
of those outside can still be place 4.

41
00:03:15.433 --> 00:03:19.900
So after 8 races place 4 is unknown,
and a ninth race is needed.

42
00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:26.200
In terms of information: about 1.4 million
possible answers, and a race gives only 720.

43
00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:31.700
That bounds it at three races, even with a clock.
Without one, the structure raises it to 9.

44
00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:36.000
The answer: 9 races, and no fewer.

45
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.967
Trap: thinking the second group's winner,
here F1, is surely place 2.

46
00:03:41.967 --> 00:03:47.433
But C2, second in the winner's group,
never raced it, and may be faster.

47
00:03:47.433 --> 00:03:51.433
The winners' race ranks groups, not horses.
So place 2 has two candidates.

48
00:03:52.233 --> 00:03:57.433
Part B: 30 horses, six per race,
and the top three. Then a plan for any case.

49
00:03:58.133 --> 00:04:03.667
Five group races,
so that every horse runs once.

50
00:04:03.667 --> 00:04:08.667
With a top three, a horse is out at three
known faster: places 4 to 6.

51
00:04:08.667 --> 00:04:13.700
Race 6: the five winners.
The track has six lanes, so one is free.

52
00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:17.700
It's tempting to add the best group's second,
but nobody knows the best group yet.

53
00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:21.700
Whatever horse we add, the adversary makes
its group not the best. The lane does not help.

54
00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:26.100
A1 won, so it is place 1.
Sort the rows by the race.

55
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:31.100
The same staircase, with i − 1 + j − 1 ≤ 2:
five candidates, A2, D1, A3, D2 and C1.

56
00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:35.900
Five fit in one race. Race 7:
D1 > C1 > A2 > A3 > D2.

57
00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:39.700
D1 is place 2 and C1 place 3.
Seven races, not six.

58
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:45.100
In general: a over b group races,
plus one for the winners.

59
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:50.600
Then c(c + 1)/2 − 1 candidates are left.
If they fit in one race, one more is enough.

60
00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:55.833
With a top four there are nine candidates,
more than six, so Part A needs two.

61
00:04:56.633 --> 00:04:59.633
Now let's write it in Python.

62
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:09.700
horses_plan takes a horses, b per race
and the top c, and race returns an order.

63
00:05:15.467 --> 00:05:19.967
Each horse gets two sets: known faster
and known slower. log keeps the races.

64
00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:27.900
beats records that x beat y.
If that is already known, nothing to do.

65
00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:38.533
The closure: everyone above x, x included,
beats everyone below y, y included.

66
00:05:48.233 --> 00:05:52.733
run races a group, keeps the order,
and records every pair: the earlier one won.

67
00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:04.700
rank: the rank is known once every other
horse is known faster or slower.

68
00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:08.700
The long line is broken in two here,
in the picture only. On the page it is one.

69
00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:19.400
First the groups: groups of b,
and every group races.

70
00:06:24.733 --> 00:06:28.400
If there is more than one group,
a race of all the winners.

71
00:06:33.733 --> 00:06:37.767
Go on while one of places
1 to c is not settled.

72
00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:51.900
pool: the open candidates, fewer than c known
faster. The fewest known faster first.

73
00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:12.700
Pick up to b horses greedily: each time the one
adding the most pairs not yet compared.

74
00:07:14.533 --> 00:07:18.567
And race them. Every such race
adds comparisons that were missing.

75
00:07:24.367 --> 00:07:28.867
At the end, return the top c by rank,
and all the races.

76
00:07:30.067 --> 00:07:35.667
Run it on the page's example: 30 horses,
six per race, top three.

77
00:07:35.667 --> 00:07:40.133
Five groups of six:
horses 0 to 5, 6 to 11, and so on.

78
00:07:40.133 --> 00:07:44.533
Race 1: group A. The order goes into log,
and every pair in it is recorded.

79
00:07:44.533 --> 00:07:47.933
And so all five groups:
five races in log.

80
00:07:47.933 --> 00:07:53.167
More than one group, so race 6:
the winners, A1 > D1 > C1 > B1 > E1.

81
00:07:53.167 --> 00:07:58.767
Place 2 is not settled, so the loop starts.
pool: the five candidates, sorted.

82
00:07:58.767 --> 00:08:04.367
chosen is empty, and picking begins.
At first no horse adds missing pairs.

83
00:08:04.367 --> 00:08:09.533
So the tie-break decides: D1, one known faster
and the most known slower.

84
00:08:09.533 --> 00:08:14.133
Then four more: A2, C1, D2 and A3.
Each adds pairs not yet compared.

85
00:08:14.133 --> 00:08:18.300
pool is empty, and race 7 runs:
D1 > C1 > A2 > A3 > D2.

86
00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:23.833
Places 1 to 3 are settled, the loop ends:
A1, D1, C1, after seven races.

87
00:08:24.633 --> 00:08:29.533
The page's box runs the same example.
After the five group races:

88
00:08:29.533 --> 00:08:33.767
Race 6, the winners. A1 is place 1,
and five candidates are left.

89
00:08:33.767 --> 00:08:38.900
And race 7 settles places 2 and 3.
On the page you can choose other a, b and c.

90
00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:45.200
The cost: each result costs O(a²) in the closure,
and memory is O(a²), two sets a horse.

91
00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:51.167
Trap: remembering that the classic puzzle ends
at 7, and answering 7 for 36 horses too.

92
00:08:51.167 --> 00:08:56.667
There five candidates fit in one race.
With a top four there are nine: two races.

93
00:08:57.463 --> 00:09:02.363
In short: count every horse's known faster,
and race only the candidates.

94
00:09:02.363 --> 00:09:07.763
Try your own numbers in the box
on question 0043's page on the site.
